    description: "Learn how to crop PDF pages and remove excess white margins online for free. Fix scanned documents, adjust print layout, and trim pages in seconds.",
    publishedAt: "2026-05-13", category: "PDF Tools", readTime: 4,
    excerpt: "Scanned pages often have thick white borders that waste space and look unprofessional. Cropping removes them without affecting the content.",
    intro: "White margins and uneven borders are a common problem in scanned PDFs and documents exported with too much padding. Cropping adjusts the visible page area so only the content is shown, making the file look cleaner for email, presentations, and printed handouts.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Why PDF Pages Have Large Margins", body: "Scanner glass captures more than the paper edge, leaving white space around the content. A4 or Letter-sized pages placed slightly off-center produce uneven margins. Documents exported from Word or InDesign with large page padding also arrive with wide borders.\n\nWhite margins are not always a problem, but when a scanned contract, report, or brochure has 2-3cm of blank border on every side, cropping improves the reading experience significantly." },
      { heading: "What Cropping Actually Does to a PDF", body: "PDF cropping sets a CropBox — a visible area definition — on each page. Content outside the CropBox is hidden, not deleted. The original page boundaries are still stored in the file structure.\n\nThis means the crop is display-level for most viewers. Some tools also allow you to trim the physical page size, which removes the hidden content permanently." },
      { heading: "How to Crop a PDF Page Online", body: "Open the ToolMint Crop PDF tool. Upload your PDF. Use the visual crop handles to drag the visible area boundaries inward from any edge. You can apply the same crop to all pages or set different crop areas for individual pages.\n\nClick Apply and download the cropped PDF. The result will have tighter, cleaner margins." },
      { heading: "Cropping to a Standard Paper Size", body: "If your scanned document has uneven margins, cropping to a consistent size makes it look professionally prepared. Set equal margins on all four sides or use preset dimensions that match the paper size your document was originally printed on.\n\nThis is especially useful before adding page numbers or watermarks, where consistent margins produce a cleaner result." },
      { heading: "Cropping vs. Rotating vs. Splitting", body: "Cropping removes space around the content. Rotating changes page orientation. Splitting divides pages into separate files.\n\nIf your document has both margin and orientation problems, rotate first, then crop. If you have a scanned booklet where two A5 pages were scanned onto one A4 sheet, split the pages visually by cropping the left and right halves separately." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Does cropping a PDF permanently delete content outside the crop area?", a: "Display-level cropping hides the content but retains it in the file. Some tools offer hard-trim options that permanently remove the data. Check which type your tool applies." },
      { q: "Can I crop all pages in a PDF to the same dimensions?", a: "Yes. Most crop tools let you apply the same crop settings to all pages at once, which saves time for scanned multi-page documents." },
      { q: "Will cropping change the file size?", a: "Slightly. If the outer areas are permanently removed, the file size decreases. Display-only cropping has minimal effect on file size." },
      { q: "Can I crop a PDF on my phone?", a: "Yes. The Crop PDF tool works in any mobile browser without installing an app." },
    ],
