    publishedAt: "2026-05-11", category: "PDF Tools", readTime: 4,
    excerpt: "Spotting changes between two contract versions or document drafts by eye is slow and error-prone. A PDF comparison tool does it in seconds.",
    intro: "When a contract comes back with small wording changes, when a report has been revised between drafts, or when you need to verify that a final document matches a reviewed version, manual comparison is unreliable. A PDF comparison tool scans both files and highlights exactly what changed.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "Why Manual PDF Comparison Fails", body: "Human reviewers miss subtle word changes, number substitutions, and clause omissions, especially in long documents. A single changed digit in a financial figure or a removed clause in a contract can have major consequences.\n\nAn automated comparison tool reads both documents at the text level and flags every insertion, deletion, and modification — including changes that are invisible to a quick scan." },
      { heading: "What a PDF Comparison Tool Detects", body: "Text additions and deletions: words, sentences, or paragraphs added or removed between versions. Formatting changes: text that was bolded, italicized, or resized. Number changes: substituted values in tables, figures, or clause references. Page changes: reordered or deleted pages.\n\nVisual comparison also detects layout shifts in image-heavy or scanned documents where text extraction is imperfect." },
      { heading: "How to Compare Two PDFs Online", body: "Open the ToolMint Compare PDF tool. Upload the original version as Document A and the updated version as Document B. Click Compare. The tool displays a side-by-side or overlay view with differences highlighted.\n\nExported results show exactly which text was changed, added, or removed with clear visual markers so you can review each change individually." },
      { heading: "Practical Uses for PDF Comparison", body: "Legal and contract review: verify that a counter-party returned the same contract with only agreed changes.\n\nAcademic review: compare a submitted draft with a revised version after feedback.\n\nCompliance and audit: verify that a final policy document matches the approved draft.\n\nProcurement: compare supplier quotations or proposals for discrepancies between versions submitted at different dates." },
      { heading: "When Comparison Does Not Work Well", body: "Scanned PDFs without OCR produce poor comparison results because the tool cannot extract searchable text. If you compare two scans, run them through PDF to Text with OCR first.\n\nSignificant layout changes — like a document reformatted from two columns to one — can make text comparison noisy because the order of extracted text changes even if the words are the same." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can I compare PDFs that were scanned, not digitally created?", a: "Yes, but OCR must process them first so the tool can extract text. Scanned PDFs without OCR only support visual comparison, which is less precise." },
      { q: "Does the comparison tool show which version is newer?", a: "No. You specify which file is the original and which is the revised version when you upload them." },
      { q: "What if the documents have the same content but different formatting?", a: "Text comparison will show no differences if words are identical. Formatting differences are shown separately in tools that support visual or structural comparison." },
      { q: "Can I compare PDFs with different page counts?", a: "Yes. The tool handles different page counts and flags pages that exist in one version but not the other." },
    ],
    relatedToolSlugs: ["compare-pdf", "redact-pdf", "pdf-to-text", "protect-pdf"],
