    slug: "how-to-convert-heic-to-jpg-free",
    title: "How to Convert HEIC to JPG for Free Online",
    metaTitle: "Convert HEIC to JPG Free Online – No App Required",
    description: "Convert iPhone HEIC photos to JPG format online for free. No app download, no signup — works in any browser on Mac, Windows, and Android.",
    publishedAt: "2026-04-23", category: "Image Tools", readTime: 4,
    excerpt: "iPhone photos save as HEIC by default, but most platforms do not accept HEIC. Here is how to convert them to JPG for free without any app.",
    intro: "Since iOS 11, iPhones save photos in HEIC format by default. HEIC is efficient and high quality, but it is not supported by Windows, most websites, many apps, or older Android devices. Converting to JPG makes iPhone photos universally compatible.",
    sections: [
      { heading: "What Is HEIC and Why iPhones Use It", body: "HEIC stands for High Efficiency Image Container. It is a format based on the HEVC video codec standard, designed to store images at half the file size of JPG with the same or better quality.\n\nApple switched to HEIC in iOS 11 because it produces smaller files, which matters when you are storing thousands of photos on a limited device. The downside is compatibility — most non-Apple software does not support HEIC natively." },
      { heading: "When HEIC Causes Problems", body: "HEIC photos are incompatible with many common workflows.", list: ["Windows 10 and earlier cannot open HEIC without a codec", "Most web upload forms reject HEIC", "WhatsApp, Instagram, and other apps accept JPEG but not HEIC", "Windows photos apps and older image editors do not support it", "E-commerce product upload portals require JPG or PNG"] },
      { heading: "How to Convert HEIC to JPG Online for Free", body: "Open the ToolMint Image Converter tool in any browser. Upload your HEIC file or multiple HEIC files together. Select JPG as the output format. Click Convert. Download the JPG files.\n\nThe conversion runs in the browser and produces standard JPG files compatible with all platforms, apps, and upload forms." },
      { heading: "Preventing HEIC on iPhone Going Forward", body: "To make your iPhone save photos as JPG instead of HEIC: open Settings, tap Camera, tap Formats, and select Most Compatible instead of High Efficiency. Future photos will save as JPG automatically.\n\nThis increases photo file size by about 50% but eliminates compatibility problems entirely. If storage is not a concern, this is the simplest long-term solution." },
      { heading: "HEIC vs. JPG: Quality and File Size", body: "HEIC at a given file size is visually comparable to JPG at roughly twice the file size. A 2MB HEIC photo has similar quality to a 4MB JPG. This makes HEIC genuinely better for storage efficiency.\n\nHowever, JPG compatibility means HEIC is rarely worth keeping for anything that will be shared outside Apple devices. Convert to JPG for sharing, keep HEIC originals only if Apple device storage is a concern." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can I open HEIC files on Windows without converting?", a: "Windows 11 can open HEIC with the HEIF Image Extensions codec installed from the Microsoft Store (free). Earlier Windows versions require conversion." },
      { q: "Does converting HEIC to JPG reduce image quality?", a: "Very slightly. HEIC is more efficient than JPG at the same quality level, so a JPG conversion at 90% quality produces a slightly larger file. At 80% quality, the visual difference is negligible for most uses." },
      { q: "Can I convert multiple HEIC photos at once?", a: "Yes. Upload multiple HEIC files together and convert all to JPG in one operation." },
      { q: "What if I cannot find the HEIC file on my iPhone?", a: "HEIC files are stored in the Photos app. Share the photo to Files or AirDrop it to another device to get the raw HEIC file for uploading to the converter." },
    ],
    relatedToolSlugs: ["image-converter", "image-compressor", "png-to-jpg", "image-resizer"],
